<h2>The course of the dispute</h2>

<p>The parties married in Delhi in 2013 and had a daughter in 2014; they separated in June 2021. The wife's complaint led to FIR No. 228/2019 at PS Nangloi under Sections 498A, 406 and 34 IPC, alleging cruelty, ill-treatment and dowry demand, and a complaint under Section 12 of the DV Act followed. The turn came at mediation: in February 2025 the parties settled all disputes for Rs 3 lakh, the DV complaint was withdrawn, and a decree of divorce by mutual consent under Section 13B of the Hindu Marriage Act was passed in July 2025. Rs 2 lakh had been paid earlier; the final demand draft of Rs 1 lakh was handed over before the High Court, and the complainant confirmed receipt of the full amount and her consent to quashing.</p>

<h2>The court's analysis</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Nature of the offence</h3><p>The wrong complained of was essentially private and personal — matrimonial discord, not a heinous offence against society that settlement cannot touch.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Settlement completed</h3><p>Divorce decreed, DV complaint withdrawn, full payment received and acknowledged in court — the compromise was performed, not merely promised.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Trial prognosis</h3><p>With the principal witness no longer supporting the prosecution, the possibility of conviction was remote and bleak.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Abuse of process</h3><p>Continuing the case would burden the parties and the court to no purpose; the FIR and all consequent proceedings were quashed against all petitioners.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Where quashing on settlement works — and where it does not</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect on quashing</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Offence private in character (498A, 406, matrimonial disputes)</td><td>Settlement weighs heavily in favour of quashing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Heinous or serious offences, offences against society</td><td>Settlement does not justify quashing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Settlement performed (payments made, decrees obtained)</td><td>Strongly supports quashing; courts prefer completed compromises</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Complainant's free, verified consent</td><td>Essential — recorded in person or through counsel before the court</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical sequencing for settling matrimonial litigation</h2>

<p>This case is a model of sequencing. Matrimonial disputes rarely consist of one proceeding: an FIR, a DV complaint, maintenance claims and divorce proceedings travel together, and a settlement that resolves one while leaving others alive resolves nothing. The mediated settlement here dealt with the whole bundle — quantum, stridhan, the divorce, the DV withdrawal — and staged the payments against each milestone, with the last instalment reserved for the quashing hearing itself. Parties negotiating a settlement should insist on that architecture: identify every pending and potential proceeding in the agreement, fix the order in which each is closed, and make the final payment contemporaneous with the final closure.</p>

<p>One caution: quashing is a discretionary power, and the High Court is not a registry for settlements. Where the record suggests coercion, where the offence has public dimensions, or where the settlement is unperformed, the petition can fail. The complainant's informed, voluntary confirmation before the court remains the irreducible requirement.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court quashing order for general information. Whether a particular FIR can be quashed on settlement depends on the nature of the allegations and the completeness of the compromise; nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
